== Chapter IV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The last principle: the usefulness of mortification — The vigor and comfort of our spiritual lives depend on our mortification — In what sense — Not absolutely and necessarily; Ps 88, Heman’s condition — Not as the next and immediate cause — As a means, by removing what is contrary — The desperate effects of any unmortified lust: it weakens the soul, Ps 38:3, 8, in various ways, and darkens it — All graces improved by the mortification of sin — The best evidence of sincerity.]

#smallcaps[The] last principle I will insist on (passing over, first, the necessity of mortification for life, and, second, the certainty of life upon mortification) is this:

III. #emph[The life, vigor, and comfort of our spiritual life depend greatly on our mortification of sin.]

Strength and comfort, power and peace, in our walk with God, are the things we desire. If any of us were seriously asked what it is that troubles us, we would have to put it under one of these heads: either we lack strength or power, vigor and life, in our obedience, in our walk with God; or we lack peace, comfort, and consolation in it. Whatever may happen to a believer that does not belong under one of these two heads does not deserve to be mentioned in the days of our complaints.

Now, all these depend greatly on a constant course of mortification. Concerning this, observe:

1\. I do not say they come from it as though they were #emph[necessarily] tied to it. A man may be kept in a constant course of mortification all his days, and yet perhaps never enjoy one good day of peace and consolation. So it was with Heman (Ps 88). His life was a life of constant mortification and walking with God, yet terrors and wounds were his portion all his days. But God singled out Heman, a choice friend, to make him an example to those who would later be in distress. Can you complain if it is no different with you than it was with Heman, that eminent servant of God? And this will be his praise to the end of the world. God makes it his prerogative to speak peace and consolation (Isa 57:18, 19). “I will do that work,” says God; “I will restore comfort to him” (verse 18). But how? By a direct work of the new creation: “I create it,” says God. The #emph[use of means] to obtain peace is ours; the #emph[giving] of it is God’s prerogative.

2\. In the ways God has instituted to give us life, vigor, courage, and consolation, mortification is not one of the immediate causes. It is the privileges of our adoption, made known to our souls, that give us these things immediately. The Spirit’s testifying “with our spirit that we are God’s children,” his giving us a new name and a white stone, adoption and justification (that is, as we sense and know them): these are the immediate causes, in the hand of the Spirit, of these things. But this I do say:

3\. In our ordinary walk with God, and in the ordinary course of his dealing with us, the vigor and comfort of our spiritual lives depend greatly on our mortification. It is not only a “causa sine qua non” [a cause without which the thing does not come about], but something that has an effectual influence on them. For:

(1.) This alone keeps sin from robbing us of both the one and the other.

Every unmortified sin will certainly do two things: \[1.\] It will #emph[weaken] the soul and rob it of its vigor. \[2.\] It will #emph[darken] the soul and rob it of its comfort and peace.

\[1.\] It #emph[weakens] the soul and robs it of its strength. When David had for a while harbored an unmortified lust in his heart, it broke all his bones and left him no spiritual strength. So he complained that he was sick, weak, wounded, faint. “There is no soundness in my body,” he says (Ps 38:3); “I am feeble and badly crushed” (verse 8); yes, “I cannot look up” (Ps 40:12). An unmortified lust will drink up the spirit and all the vigor of the soul, and weaken it for all duties. For:

1#emph[st]. It #emph[disorders] and unsettles the heart itself, by entangling its affections. It turns the heart away from the spiritual frame that is required for vigorous communion with God. It lays hold on the affections and makes its object loved and desirable, and so drives out the love of the Father (1 John 2:15; 3:17). The soul then cannot say honestly and truly to God, “You are my portion,” for it has something else that it loves. Fear, desire, and hope, the choice affections of the soul, which should be full of God, will in one way or another be entangled with it.

2#emph[nd]. It fills the #emph[thoughts] with schemes about it. Thoughts are the great suppliers of the soul, bringing in provision to satisfy its affections. If sin remains unmortified in the heart, they must again and again be making provision for the flesh, to fulfill its lusts. They must polish, adorn, and dress up the objects of the flesh, and bring them home to give satisfaction. And in the service of a defiled imagination they are able to do this beyond all expression.

3#emph[rd]. It breaks out and actually hinders duty. The ambitious man must be scheming, the worldly man must be working or scheming, and the sensual, vain person must be providing himself with vanity, when they should be engaged in the worship of God.

If it were my present business to set out the breaches, ruin, weakness, and desolation that one unmortified lust will bring on a soul, this discourse would have to be extended far beyond what I intend.

\[2.\] As sin #emph[weakens], so it #emph[darkens] the soul. It is a cloud, a thick cloud, that spreads itself over the face of the soul and cuts off all the beams of God’s love and favor. It takes away all sense of the privilege of our adoption; and if the soul begins to gather up thoughts of consolation, sin quickly scatters them. Of this, more later.

Now, in this respect the vigor and power of our spiritual life depend on our mortification: it is the only means of removing what will allow us neither the one nor the other. Men who are sick and wounded under the power of lust try many ways of getting help. They cry to God when their perplexed thoughts overwhelm them; even to God they cry, but are not delivered. In vain they try many remedies; “there is no healing.” So in Hos 5:13, “Ephraim saw his sickness and Judah his wound,” and they tried various remedies. Nothing will do until they come (verse 15) to “admit their guilt.” Men may see their sickness and wounds; but if they do not apply the right remedies, they will not be cured.

(2.) Mortification prunes all the graces of God and makes room for them to grow in our hearts. The life and vigor of our spiritual lives consist in the vigor and flourishing of the plants of grace in our hearts. Now, you may see this in a garden. Let a precious herb be planted, and let the ground be left untilled and weeds grow around it. Perhaps it will still live, but it will be a poor, withering, useless thing. You must look and search for it, and sometimes can hardly find it; and when you do, you can hardly tell whether it is the plant you are looking for or not; and suppose it is, you can make no use of it at all. But let another of the same kind be set in the ground, naturally as barren and poor as the other, and let it be well weeded and everything harmful and hurtful removed from it: it flourishes and thrives. You can see it at your first look into the garden, and have it for your use whenever you please. So it is with the graces of the Spirit that are planted in our hearts. It is true, they #emph[are] still there; they remain in a heart where mortification is somewhat neglected. But they are “about to die” (Rev 3:2); they are withering and decaying. The heart is like the sluggard’s field, so overgrown with weeds that you can hardly see the good grain. Such a man may search for faith, love, and zeal, and hardly be able to find any. And if he does find that these graces are still there, alive and sincere, yet they are so weak, so clogged with lusts, that they are of very little use. They remain, indeed, but are ready to die. But now let the heart be cleansed by mortification. Let the weeds of lust be constantly and daily rooted up (as they spring up daily, nature being their proper soil). Let room be made for grace to thrive and flourish. How every grace will then play its part, and be ready for every use and purpose!

(3.) As to our peace: just as nothing has any evidence of sincerity without it, so I know of nothing that has such evidence of sincerity in it, and that is no small foundation of our peace. Mortification is the soul’s vigorous opposition to self, and in it sincerity is most evident.
